<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
A cryptographic key or passphrase that is embedded in source code can be recovered
by anyone with access to the source code or to the compiled binary. An attacker who
recovers the key can decrypt data that it protects, or forge ciphertexts and
authentication tags that the application will accept as genuine.
</p>
<p>
This query finds constant values (literals, constant arrays, and repeated-value
arrays) that flow to the key or passphrase argument of a cryptographic API. It
complements the standard <code>rust/hard-coded-cryptographic-value</code> query by
recognizing additional APIs from the <code>age</code>, <code>orion</code> and
<code>ring</code> crates, and keys that are wrapped with
<code>GenericArray::from</code> or <code>secrecy::Secret::new</code> before use.
Results that the standard query already reports are not repeated.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Do not embed keys or passphrases in source code. Generate keys with a
cryptographically secure random number generator, or load them at runtime from a
protected secret store such as a key management service, and rotate any key that
has been committed to source control.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, an AES key is a fixed array of zero bytes:
</p>
<sample src="HardcodedCryptographicKeyBad.rs" />
<p>
In the following corrected example, the key is supplied at runtime from a protected
secret store:
</p>
<sample src="HardcodedCryptographicKeyGood.rs" />
</example>

<references>
<li>
MITRE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/321.html">CWE-321: Use of Hard-coded Cryptographic Key</a>.
</li>
<li>
OWASP: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Key_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html">Key Management Cheat Sheet</a>.
</li>
</references>

</qhelp>
